/**
 * LUNG CANCER: LIVING WITH IT, CARE AND DECISIONS (UK and NHS focus). Facts checked 25 September 2026.
 *
 * The patient side of the lung record, written to the colorectal and pancreatic standard
 * (./colorectal-living.ts, ./pancreatic-living.ts): nine decision rows on the non-small-cell record and one on the
 * small-cell record, each of which the decisions page turns into a section (surgery, radiotherapy or both for early
 * disease and what lung function means for the choice; treatment before or after surgery; chemoradiotherapy then a
 * year of immunotherapy for locally advanced disease; what a biomarker result changes once the cancer has spread and
 * why waiting for it is usually right; which immunotherapy by PD-L1; what to do when a targeted drug stops working,
 * including the second biopsy; cancer that has spread to the brain; a trial or standard treatment; when to talk about
 * palliative care, which in lung cancer has randomised evidence for starting early and is presented that way; and for
 * small-cell disease, why the timeline is faster and what that means for the decisions in front of you). It also
 * carries the hand-written question sets the appointment sheet, prep pack and decision pages read (exported as
 * `nsclcQuestions` and `sclcQuestions`, wired in src/data/questions.ts) and living-with material attached as sourced
 * notes and links to the symptom, procedure and support records other files own (breathlessness and what actually
 * helps, cough, pain, fatigue, appetite, pneumonitis from immunotherapy and from radiotherapy, the rash and diarrhoea
 * from the targeted drugs, brain symptoms, pleural effusion and drainage, oxygen at home, stopping smoking offered
 * without blame, and the weight of stigma).
 *
 * On stigma, which is part of the clinical picture in this cancer and not a footnote. Lung cancer is the cancer
 * people are most often asked "did you smoke?" about, and the question lands on people who smoked, people who gave up
 * decades ago and people who never smoked at all. Nothing here treats a diagnosis as a consequence: the smoking
 * material is written as a treatment recommendation with a named benefit (NICE NG122 1.3.1 to 1.3.3), never as a
 * verdict, and the emotional material says plainly that nobody has to earn their treatment. Where a sentence could be
 * read as blame it has been rewritten.
 *
 * This file creates no glossary terms: the glossary pass (agent A) owns them. Drafts for the terms this material
 * wants are exported as `lungLivingTermDrafts`, written to the glossary standard so they can be registered by
 * spreading the array; the ids they use are referenced nowhere in the graph until then. Trials are owned by the
 * trials pass (agent B): no trial record is created here, and the rows reference only trials the corpus already has.
 *
 * Sources, all read 25 September 2026: NICE NG122 (lung cancer: diagnosis and management, 2019, last updated March
 * 2024) and NICE NG234 (spinal metastases and metastatic spinal cord compression, 2023); the NHS lung cancer,
 * sepsis, coughing up blood, quit smoking, carers and clinical trials pages; Roy Castle Lung Cancer Foundation;
 * Macmillan; Cancer Research UK; Asthma + Lung UK; Maggie's; Marie Curie; and Temel et al. (NEJM 2010) for early
 * palliative care. Nothing here is advice for an individual and every page says so.
 */

