const draft = (x: Omit<TermInput, "kind" | "asOf">): TermInput => ({ kind: "term", asOf, cancers: BOTH, ...x });
export const lungLivingTermDrafts: TermInput[] = [
  draft({ id: "breathlessness-in-cancer", name: "Breathlessness (dyspnoea) and what helps", category: "Side effects", aka: ["dyspnoea", "shortness of breath", "breathing control", "handheld fan", "recovery breathing", "pursed lips breathing", "breathlessness clinic"],
    tldr: "Breathlessness in lung cancer has separate, separately treatable causes: a chest infection, anxiety, anaemia, fluid around the lung, a partly blocked airway, muscle weakness, lung surgery, and inflammation after radiotherapy or immunotherapy. The things with the best evidence behind them are unglamorous: a handheld fan pointed at the face, relaxed tummy breathing, a forward lean position, pacing, and referral to a breathlessness service.",
    summary: `NICE NG122 (1.15.3) says to consider non-pharmacological interventions based on psychosocial support, breathing control and coping strategies for people with breathlessness, and (1.15.4) that these should be delivered by a multidisciplinary group coordinated by a professional with an interest in breathlessness and expertise in the techniques, for example a nurse, physiotherapist or occupational therapist, and that although this may be provided in a breathlessness clinic people should have access to it in all care settings.
Cancer Research UK lists the causes that should each be looked for rather than assumed: a chest infection (coughing up coloured phlegm, a high temperature; contact the GP or specialist nurse because antibiotics may be needed); anxiety, which speeds the breathing and feeds itself; anaemia, because haemoglobin carries oxygen; lung surgery, after which some breathlessness is normal and may or may not settle; a pleural effusion, treatable by draining the fluid; muscle weakness, often from cachexia; a partly blocked airway, treatable with a stent or with heat; a pericardial effusion; radiation pneumonitis, acute or chronic; and rarely lung changes from chemotherapy, immunotherapy or targeted drugs.
The techniques, from Cancer Research UK and Asthma + Lung UK. Relaxed tummy breathing (breathing control): practise when you are not breathless, for 10 minutes at a time at least twice a day, sitting supported with shoulders and upper chest relaxed and one hand on the tummy, feeling it rise and fall. Recovery breathing, for when you are too breathless for that: a fan blowing cool air across the face, a supported forward lean such as resting your arms on a kitchen counter, and a focus on long relaxed breaths out. Pursed lips breathing: in through the nose, out through narrowed lips, which helps the air leave and makes room for the next breath. Moving around: take your time, match your breathing to your steps, do not hold your breath when climbing or bending, and blow out on effort. Planning: keep what you need on one floor, use a trolley, gather everything before you start a task, and pace with rest built in.
Asthma + Lung UK adds the point that reassures most people: evidence suggests lung function tests and scans do not always show how breathless you feel, because the way you breathe, your lifestyle and how you think about breathing also affect it. Macmillan says to contact your doctor straight away if breathlessness is a new symptom, if you have pain when you breathe, or if it gets worse quickly, and that if you cannot speak to your doctor and it keeps worsening, go straight to A&E.`,
    links: [CRUK_BREATHLESSNESS, ALUK_BREATHLESSNESS, ALUK_MANAGE, MAC_BREATHLESSNESS, NG122_PALLIATIVE, CRUK_BREATHING_TREATMENT, CRUK_OXYGEN_HOME], terms: ["pleural-effusion", "anaemia", "cachexia", "radiation-pneumonitis"], technologies: ["palliative-care", "cbt-fatigue-distress", "exercise-during-chemotherapy"] }),

  draft({ id: "pneumonitis-lung-cancer", name: "Pneumonitis: lung inflammation from immunotherapy and from radiotherapy", category: "Side effects", aka: ["immune pneumonitis", "radiation pneumonitis", "interstitial lung disease", "drug-induced lung injury", "ILD"],
    tldr: "Two of the main lung cancer treatments can inflame the lung they are treating. Immunotherapy pneumonitis can begin at any time during treatment or months after the last dose; radiotherapy pneumonitis usually begins in the weeks after treatment, and a smaller number of people get a later, longer-lasting version. The warning signs are the same and so is the rule: breathlessness, a new or worsening cough, wheezing or a fever over 37.5 C means ringing the 24-hour number the same day.",
    summary: `Macmillan's pages for pembrolizumab, durvalumab and atezolizumab all carry the same sentence: this treatment can cause inflammation of the lungs, called pneumonitis, and you should contact the hospital straight away on the 24-hour number if you notice breathlessness, a cough that does not go away, wheezing, or a fever with a temperature over 37.5 C, during treatment or after it ends. Macmillan also says immunotherapy reactions can start after treatment has finished, which is why the patient alert card matters and why an out-of-hours doctor needs to be told what you have had.
Cancer Research UK describes the radiotherapy version: radiotherapy to the chest area might cause some inflammation of the lungs, so soon after treatment you might have a dry cough or shortness of breath, which is acute radiation pneumonitis; in a small number of people a cough and breathlessness continue because of changes in the lung tissue called chronic radiation pneumonitis, which might start many months or a few years after treatment.
Why this matters more in lung cancer than elsewhere. Many people here have had both chest radiotherapy and immunotherapy (NICE NG122 1.6.18 recommends durvalumab after concurrent chemoradiation for locally advanced disease with PD-L1 on 1 percent or more of tumour cells, and 1.10.6 recommends it after chemoradiotherapy for limited-stage small-cell disease), and many also have another lung condition. That makes new breathlessness genuinely ambiguous, and the ambiguity is exactly why it is reported rather than watched: a chest infection, a pleural effusion, a clot and pneumonitis are treated completely differently and cannot be told apart at home.
What not to do: do not assume a new cough is the cancer, and do not assume it is nothing because you feel otherwise well. Treatment for pneumonitis usually means steroids and a pause in the drug, and it works better started early.`,
    links: [MAC_PEMBRO, MAC_DURVA, MAC_ATEZO, CRUK_BREATHLESSNESS, NG122_MANAGEMENT], terms: ["radiation-pneumonitis", "irae", "pleural-effusion"], technologies: ["checkpoint-inhibitor", "sbrt"] }),

  draft({ id: "urgent-help-lung-cancer", name: "When to seek urgent help with lung cancer (NHS 111 and 999)", category: "Clinical", aka: ["haemoptysis", "superior vena cava obstruction", "neutropenic sepsis lung cancer", "breathless at rest", "spinal cord compression"],
    tldr: "Call 999 for struggling to breathe, coughing up more than a few spots or streaks of blood, signs of sepsis, or new weakness, numbness or bladder and bowel problems with back pain. Ring the hospital's 24-hour line the same day for a temperature above 37.5 C or below 36 C, feeling unwell with a normal temperature, new or worsening breathlessness or cough, or swelling of the face, neck or arms. Use 111 or an urgent GP appointment for a few flecks of blood in phlegm.",
    summary: `The wording, situation by situation, from the pages it comes from.
Breathing. The NHS lung cancer symptoms page says to call 999 or go to A&E if you are struggling to breathe, choking, gasping and unable to speak, or if you have pain in your chest or upper back. Cancer Research UK says that if you suddenly become breathless or your breathing gets worse with a pleural effusion, call 999 or go to A&E straight away, as you may need urgent treatment. Macmillan says to contact your doctor straight away if breathlessness is a new symptom, if you have pain when you breathe, or if it gets worse quickly, and to go to A&E if you cannot reach them and it keeps worsening.
Coughing up blood. The NHS says to ask for an urgent GP appointment or get help from NHS 111 if you have coughed up a few small spots, flecks or streaks of blood or noticed blood in your phlegm, and to call 999 or go to A&E immediately if you are coughing up more than just a few spots or streaks, or if you are coughing up blood and finding it hard to breathe, have a very fast heartbeat or have pain in your chest or upper back.
Infection. Macmillan says to contact the hospital straight away on the 24-hour number for a temperature above 37.5 C, a temperature below 36 C, or feeling unwell even with a normal temperature. The NHS says to call 999 or go to A&E for breathing very fast, confusion or slurred speech or not making sense, blue, pale or blotchy skin, a very high or very low temperature or feeling hot, cold or shivery, or a rash that does not fade when pressed.
Swelling of the face, neck or arms. Macmillan says most cases of superior vena cava obstruction are caused by lung cancer, that symptoms can develop over days or weeks, that they include breathlessness, headaches or fullness in the head that are worse leaning forward, facial swelling with changes to your complexion, changes in eyesight, a swollen neck, swollen arms and hands, visible swollen veins on the chest and dizziness, and that if you have any of these it is important to contact your cancer doctor or nurse straight away because it needs to be treated quickly.
Back pain with weakness or numbness. NICE NG234 treats bladder or bowel dysfunction, difficulty walking, limb weakness, numbness or altered sensation and radicular pain in someone with a past or current diagnosis of cancer as an oncological emergency requiring immediate contact with the coordinating service, and says severe unremitting or progressive back pain, pain aggravated by standing, sitting, moving, coughing, sneezing or straining, night pain disturbing sleep and localised tenderness should prompt advice within 24 hours.
Brain symptoms. Cancer Research UK lists memory problems, mood or personality changes, seizures, confusion, severe headaches often with sickness, and weakness of an arm or leg as symptoms of cancer that has spread to the brain.`,
    links: [NHS_LUNG_SYMPTOMS, NHS_COUGHING_BLOOD, NHS_SEPSIS, MAC_SVCO, MAC_BREATHLESSNESS, CRUK_PLEURAL, NG234, NHS_111, NHS_999], terms: ["febrile-neutropenia", "neutropenia", "pleural-effusion", "brain-metastases", "bone-metastases"] }),

  draft({ id: "smoking-and-lung-cancer-support", name: "Smoking, stopping smoking and lung cancer treatment (without blame)", category: "Clinic basics", aka: ["stop smoking after diagnosis", "smoking cessation lung cancer", "nicotine replacement therapy", "lung cancer stigma", "never-smoker lung cancer"],
    tldr: "Stopping smoking after a lung cancer diagnosis is offered as a treatment with a named benefit: NICE says smoking raises the risk of lung complications after surgery and that people should be advised to stop as soon as the diagnosis is suspected, and in the same breath that surgery must not be postponed to let people stop. Nothing about your care depends on whether you succeed, and lung cancer also happens to people who never smoked.",
    summary: `NICE NG122 makes three statements in a row and all three matter. 1.3.1: inform people that smoking increases the risk of pulmonary complications after lung cancer surgery. 1.3.2: advise people to stop smoking as soon as the diagnosis of lung cancer is suspected and tell them why this is important. 1.3.3: do not postpone surgery for lung cancer to allow people to stop smoking. The third is the one that is rarely read out loud, and it is the one that answers the fear most people have, which is that treatment is conditional.
Roy Castle Lung Cancer Foundation says it is equally important to quit if you have already been diagnosed, because research suggests this can help you respond better to treatment, and lists what is available: local stop smoking services in England, Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland; vapes, which it describes as now one of the most effective tools for quitting, not available on NHS prescription so obtained from a specialist vape shop or a stop smoking service; nicotine replacement in patches, gum, inhalators, microtabs, lozenges and nasal sprays, usually over 8 to 12 weeks; and stop smoking medicines that manage withdrawal. It also runs a Quit Support forum. NHS Better Health provides a free Personal Quit Plan and says reaching 28 days smoke free makes you 5 times more likely to quit for good.
On stigma, plainly. Lung cancer is the cancer people are most often asked about their smoking history, and the question reaches everyone: people who smoke, people who stopped decades ago, and people who never smoked, in whom the record notes some targetable changes such as ROS1 rearrangement are enriched (Bergethon 2012). The measurable harm of the stigma is that it delays people seeing a doctor and makes them less likely to ask for help afterwards. Cancer Research UK lists guilt among the common feelings after a lung cancer diagnosis and says specialist nurses are usually the first point of call if you are finding it difficult to cope; Maggie's centres and Roy Castle's nurse line are open whatever your history.
Two sentences worth keeping. Nobody has to earn their treatment. If you smoke and do not want to stop, the offer stays open and your care does not change.`,
    links: [NG122_MANAGEMENT, ROY_CASTLE_QUIT, NHS_QUIT, CRUK_COPING, MAGGIES], terms: ["performance-status"], technologies: ["psycho-oncology", "prehabilitation", "peer-support-groups"] }),

  draft({ id: "oxygen-at-home-cancer", name: "Oxygen at home and when travelling", category: "Clinic basics", aka: ["home oxygen", "oxygen concentrator", "portable oxygen", "oxygen on holiday"],
    tldr: "Oxygen is prescribed for low blood oxygen, not for the feeling of breathlessness, which is why some very breathless people are told they do not need it and are offered a fan and breathing techniques instead. Where it is needed it can be delivered to the home, and it can be arranged for travel and holidays with notice.",
    summary: `Cancer Research UK says that if you are very breathless you can have oxygen tanks brought to you at home, and keeps separate pages on organising oxygen at home and on getting oxygen when you are travelling or on holiday. It also puts oxygen in its proper place in the list of things that help: alongside breathing and relaxation techniques, a handheld fan, treating the cause (a chest infection, anaemia, a pleural effusion, a blocked airway) and referral to a breathlessness clinic.
The distinction that causes the most upset is between low oxygen in the blood, which oxygen treats, and the sensation of breathlessness, which it often does not. NICE NG122 (1.15.3 and 1.15.4) recommends non-pharmacological interventions based on psychosocial support, breathing control and coping strategies for breathlessness, delivered by a multidisciplinary group with expertise in the techniques and available in all care settings. Asthma + Lung UK makes the same point from the other direction: lung function tests and scans do not always show how breathless you feel.
Practical questions worth asking: who assesses whether I need it, what happens to my electricity bill with a concentrator, how much notice does a holiday need, and is there a fan and a breathlessness service as well.`,
    links: [CRUK_OXYGEN_HOME, CRUK_BREATHLESSNESS, CRUK_BREATHING_TREATMENT, NG122_PALLIATIVE, ALUK_BREATHLESSNESS], terms: ["anaemia", "pleural-effusion"] }),

  draft({ id: "carers-lung-cancer-uk", name: "Carers: what you can do with lung cancer, and UK carer support", category: "Clinic basics", aka: ["caring for someone with lung cancer", "carer's assessment", "helping someone who is breathless", "carer's allowance"],
    tldr: "You are a carer if you look after someone regularly because they are ill, and washing, dressing, medicines, lifts to appointments, shopping, bills and sitting with someone all count. The lung-specific skills are worth learning early: how to help someone through an episode of breathlessness, which changes mean ringing the team, and how to talk about smoking without it becoming an argument.",
    summary: `What you can do in the moment. Cancer Research UK's breathing advice works as instructions for a second person: point a handheld fan so a stream of cool air crosses their face, help them into a supported forward lean (arms resting on a counter or on pillows on a bed table), and coach long relaxed breaths out until the breathing settles rather than urging them to take a big breath in. Macmillan has a section on looking after someone with breathlessness and notes that anxiety and breathlessness feed each other, so a calm voice is doing real work.
What to watch for. The red cards on the lung record list breathlessness at rest or struggling to breathe, coughing up more than a few spots or streaks of blood, a temperature above 37.5 C or below 36 C or feeling unwell with a normal temperature, signs of sepsis, swelling of the face, neck or arms, and new weakness, numbness or bladder or bowel problems with back pain. Macmillan says to ask the cancer doctor or specialist nurse who to contact, how to contact them at night, on bank holidays and at weekends, and when to call.
What you are entitled to. The NHS says to contact adult social services at your local council and ask for a carer's assessment, that you are a carer if you look after someone regularly because they are ill, that help with washing, dressing, medicines, travel to appointments, shopping, cleaning, laundry, bills and finances counts, as does sitting with someone for company or watching over them, that the assessment usually lasts at least an hour and can be face to face, by phone or online, and that you can bring someone with you or use an advocate. It lists Carers UK (0808 808 7777), Age UK and Independent Age helplines, and keeps a separate page on the benefits carers may be able to claim.
Who supports you. Roy Castle Lung Cancer Foundation's support is open to everyone affected by lung cancer, not only to patients, with a nurse-staffed telephone line, information days, support groups and an online community; Maggie's centres are free, need no appointment and are open to family and friends. Cancer Research UK says taking someone to appointments to write things down and remind the person what they wanted to ask is one of the most useful things a carer does.`,
    links: [NHS_CARER_ASSESSMENT, NHS_CARER_BENEFITS, CRUK_BREATHLESSNESS, MAC_BREATHLESSNESS, ROY_CASTLE_SUPPORT, MAGGIES, CRUK_COPING], terms: ["urgent-help-lung-cancer", "breathlessness-in-cancer"] }),

  draft({ id: "waiting-for-biomarker-results-lung", name: "Waiting for the biomarker result before starting treatment", category: "Genomics & genetics", aka: ["molecular testing lung cancer", "EGFR ALK result", "National Genomics Test Directory", "turnaround time", "repeat biopsy"],
    tldr: "In advanced lung cancer the tumour gene panel usually decides between daily tablets, immunotherapy and chemotherapy, and the three are not interchangeable. That is why a short wait for the result is generally the better decision, and why the useful questions are which panel was sent, when it is due back, and what would make your team start sooner.",
    summary: `NICE NG122 (1.2.12) says to see the National Genomics Test Directory for guidance on next-generation sequencing panels to guide treatment, and (1.2.11) that when taking samples teams should ensure they are adequate, without unacceptable risk to the person, to permit pathological diagnosis including tumour subtyping and assessment of molecular markers. Those two sentences are the whole of the guideline's position, and they put the emphasis where it belongs: on getting enough tissue, once.
Why waiting is usually right. The genes with a licensed treatment attached in lung cancer include EGFR, ALK, ROS1, KRAS G12C, BRAF, MET, RET, HER2 and NTRK, and for most of them the targeted drug outperforms what would otherwise be given first. Starting immunotherapy before an EGFR or ALK result is back is also not neutral: the guideline itself restricts some checkpoint recommendations to disease without EGFR mutations or ALK rearrangements (for example 1.6.9 for durvalumab before and after surgery). A treatment started in the wrong lane is harder to undo than a fortnight of waiting.
When waiting is not right. If you are unwell, losing weight fast or in pain, a team may reasonably start something now and change later. That is a judgement about your condition, not a failure, and the question to ask is what they would start with and how quickly they would switch.
What to ask, in order: which panel was sent and when; is there enough tissue for all of it; would a blood test for tumour DNA give an answer sooner; if the sample was inadequate, is the plan a repeat biopsy or a blood test; and what would you start today if we could not wait. The NHS explains that your hospital specialist requests genomic testing, usually on a sample already taken, and who sees the result.`,
    links: [NG122_DIAGNOSIS, NHS_GENOMIC, NHS_LUNG_TESTS, MAC_NSCLC], terms: ["ngs", "ctdna", "egfr", "alk"], technologies: ["liquid-biopsy", "multidisciplinary-tumour-board"] }),

  draft({ id: "when-a-targeted-drug-stops-working", name: "When a targeted drug stops working, and the second biopsy", category: "Clinical", aka: ["acquired resistance", "re-biopsy", "oligoprogression", "histological transformation", "MET amplification"],
    tldr: "Targeted tablets work until the cancer finds a way round them, which it does in most people eventually. Growth in one spot while everything else stays quiet is often treated locally with the tablet continued; growth in several places usually means a change. A repeat sample, of tissue or of blood, can show what changed, and sometimes shows the cancer has changed type, which no blood test can see.",
    summary: `What resistance looks like. A scan showing one growing area with everything else stable is not the same event as several new areas, and teams often treat the first with local radiotherapy and keep the tablet going. Asking which pattern yours is turns a frightening scan report into a decision with two named options.
Why a second sample is offered. NICE NG122 (1.2.11) asks teams to take samples adequate to permit pathological diagnosis including tumour subtyping and assessment of molecular markers, and (1.2.13) to choose investigations that give the most information about diagnosis and staging with the least risk to the person, thinking carefully before performing a test that gives only diagnostic pathology when other information is also needed. Applied at resistance, that is the argument for and against a repeat biopsy in one sentence: it can find a new resistance change or show that the cancer has transformed into a different type, which changes the whole treatment plan, and it is a procedure with its own risks.
Tissue or blood. A blood test for tumour DNA is quicker, easier and can find a new mutation. It cannot see a change in how the cancer looks under the microscope, which is the one finding that a tissue sample uniquely gives. So the honest framing is: what question are we asking, and which test can answer it.
What comes next. Options recorded for lung cancer after a targeted drug include a next-generation drug against the same target, adding or switching to chemotherapy, antibody drug conjugates such as patritumab deruxtecan in the HERTHENA-Lung02 setting, and trials. The NHS says you can ask your doctor or a patient organisation about clinical trials you may be eligible for, and trials usually need you to be well enough, so the referral conversation belongs at this point rather than later.`,
    links: [NG122_DIAGNOSIS, NHS_TRIALS, MAC_OSIMERTINIB, NHS_LUNG_TESTS], terms: ["ctdna", "ngs", "brain-metastases"], technologies: ["liquid-biopsy", "sbrt", "multidisciplinary-tumour-board"] }),
];

